<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">J. J. Thomson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Joseph John Thomson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Joseph John Thomson was a British physicist, born at Cheetham Hill in Manchester, who in 1897 showed that cathode rays were made of previously unknown negatively charged particles, now called electrons: the first subatomic particle to be discovered. Appointed Cavendish Professor of Physics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> in 1884, he received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics in 1906 for his work on the conduction of electricity by gases. In 1912 he found the first evidence for isotopes of a stable element, work with Francis William Aston that led to the mass spectrograph. Seven of his students won Nobel Prizes, and he was Master of Trinity College from 1918 until his death in 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/j-j-thomson.jpg" alt="J. J. Thomson" width="330" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not Mentioned · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J.J_Thomson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thomson's mother, Emma Swindells, came from a local textile family, and his father, Joseph James Thomson, ran an antiquarian bookshop founded by Thomson's great-grandfather. He was a reserved but devout Anglican. In 1870, at the unusually young age of 14, he was admitted to Owens College in Manchester, where Balfour Stewart introduced him to physical research. His parents planned an engineering apprenticeship, but his father's death in 1873 ended the plan. In 1876 he moved to Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, where he graduated in 1880 as Second Wrangler in the Tripos and became a Fellow of Trinity the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 22 December 1884 he was appointed Cavendish Professor of Physics, to considerable surprise, since candidates such as Osborne Reynolds and Richard Glazebrook were older and more experienced in laboratory work. He published on the electromagnetic theory of Maxwell, introduced the concept of the electromagnetic mass of a charged particle, and wrote textbooks, among them Notes on Recent Researches in Electricity and Magnetism (1893), sometimes called the third volume of Maxwell. Thomson was knighted in 1908, received the Order of Merit in 1912 and became Master of Trinity College in 1918. He chaired the committee on natural science in education whose 1918 report was known as the Thomson Report.</p><p>Thomson made his suggestion about a particle more than 1,000 times lighter than a hydrogen atom on 30 April 1897, after finding that cathode rays travelled much further through air than an atom-sized particle should. In May and June he deflected the rays by an electric field in a tube with a better vacuum, which earlier experimenters had failed to do because their tubes held too much gas. By comparing electric and magnetic deflection he measured the mass-to-charge ratio. He called the particles corpuscles and used the word electron only by 1914. In 1904 he proposed the plum pudding model of the atom. In 1912, with Francis William Aston, he passed neon ions through electric and magnetic fields and found two isotopes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Thomson died on 30 August 1940, and his ashes rest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> near the graves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-rutherford">Ernest Rutherford</a>. Rutherford succeeded him as Cavendish Professor and showed that the positive charge of the atom is concentrated in a nucleus, which disproved the plum pudding model. His son George Paget Thomson won the 1937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics for proving the wave-like properties of electrons. Thomson married Rose Elisabeth Paget in 1890, and they had two children. The thomson, a unit of mass-to-charge ratio in mass spectrometry, was proposed in his honour in 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “J. J. Thomson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
